1, "poll_margin": null, "poll_sd": null, "poll_weight": 0.0, "fundamentals": {"margin": -1.9, "lean": -7.0, "environment": 8.3, "incumbency": -2.0, "money": -1.2}, "rating_margin": -12.5, "sigma_race": 6.0, "sigma_total": 6.69, "baseline_margin": -8.26, "analyst_adjustment": 0.26, "key_factors": ["R+7 district with incumbent Pete Stauber running for reelection", "Dominant fundraising edge: ~$2.0M raised and $1.2M on hand vs. ~$317K and $90K for Swanson, with no meaningful outside spending either way", "Expert raters unanimously call the race Safe or Likely R, and prediction markets give Democrats only about a 12% chance", "A Democratic-favorable national environment is the main force pulling the margin closer"], "rationale": "Stauber combines a seven-point Republican district lean with a lopsided money advantage and no significant outside opposition, while every major rater and the prediction market treat him as a clear favorite. The Democratic-leaning national environment keeps the margin from reaching Solid R territory, but not enough to threaten the incumbent.", "watch": "A stronger-than-expected Democratic wave could compress the margin, since fundamentals alone put the district near even in this environment.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings.", "Incumbent Pete Stauber (R) is running."]}, "overview": "Pete Stauber, the Republican incumbent in Minnesota's 8th District, enters November as a clear favorite over DFL nominee Trina Swanson. The district leans Republican by about seven points, and Stauber holds a commanding financial edge, having raised roughly $2 million to Swanson's $317,000, with more than $1.2 million on hand against her $90,000. Virtually no outside spending has entered the race on either side.\n\nExpert raters uniformly place the contest in the Safe or Likely Republican column, and prediction markets give Democrats only about a one-in-eight chance. Headlines de